    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, idiomatic replication of the existing `RequireUniqueTelephone` uniqueness feature, and it lands cleanly in this codebase's conventions:\n\n- **Right unit, right place.** The uniqueness check is its own `Require<X>` function step (one public method), the checked exception lives in `rest/escalation/`, the handler responds via `ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<...>>`, and the escalation YAML maps the exception to the handler \u2014 exactly matching the `DuplicateTelephone*` / `DuplicateHousehold*` trio.\n- **No duplication introduced.** It reuses the existing `EmailNormalizer.isBlank/normalize` helpers rather than re-implementing normalization, and reuses `NormalizeOwnerEmail` as the upstream normalizer. The structural parallel with `RequireUniqueTelephone` (each with its own `findAll` loop) is the established idiom here, not copy-paste that should be extracted \u2014 OfficeFloor's one-public-method-per-class rule makes a shared base awkward, and the surrounding `Require*` classes already accept this shape.\n- **Wiring is consistent.** `uniqueEmail` is inserted right after `email` (normalize) and before `registrationDate`, with `govern: [ transaction ]` like every sibling step, and placed after normalization so both sides compare in canonical form.\n- **No catch-all growth.** Each new class carries a single responsibility; nothing existing is being overloaded.\n\nMinor, non-blocking observation (not requesting changes): since `NormalizeOwnerEmail` already writes the lower-cased value back onto the shared DTO, `RequireUniqueEmail` re-normalizing the incoming value via `EmailNormalizer.normalize(email)` is slightly redundant (the telephone version reads the already-normalized field directly). It's harmless and arguably more defensive, so it doesn't warrant a change.",
